<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trento</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Trent, Tridentum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trento is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adige">Adige</a> river in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a>. It began as the Roman town of Tridentum, became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> duchy and from 1027 was ruled by prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> met there between 1545 and 1563. Austrian from 1803, it passed to Italy after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trento.jpg" alt="Trento" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BelPatty86 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trento_visione_d%27insieme_6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Trento stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adige">Adige</a> river, on the river route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolzano">Bolzano</a> and the low Alpine passes of the Brenner and the Reschen Pass. It is the capital of the autonomous province of Trento, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a>, in the region of Trentino-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">Alto Adige</a>/Südtirol, and has 119,359 inhabitants. The city centre lies just under 200 metres above sea level, while the municipality reaches 2,000 metres at Monte Bondone. Before the river was diverted in the mid-nineteenth century the Adige ran through the centre of the town and was a navigable commercial route in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Its old course is now covered by Via Torre Vanga, Via Torre Verde and Via Alessandro Manzoni.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The origins of Trento are uncertain. Some scholars hold that it was a Rhaetian settlement, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adige">Adige</a> area was influenced by neighbours including the Veneti, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>. Other theories say the Gauls founded the city in the fourth century BCE. A late-Roman basilica lies beneath the later cathedral, which can be seen in an underground crypt. The Castello del Buonconsiglio was the seat of the Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a> of Trento, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> was held in the city in the sixteenth century. It was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> annexed it in 1919.</p><p>The railway station opened in 1859 on the Brenner line from Verona to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a>, the main rail link between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The Teatro Sociale was built in 1819. The University of Trento was founded in 1962 as a Higher University Institute of Social Sciences.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Much of the Late Medieval and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> centre has been restored to its pastel colours and wooden balconies. Part of the medieval walls and a circular tower can be seen in Piazza Fiera. The Romanesque-Gothic Cathedral of Saint Vigilius dates from the twelfth and thirteenth centuries, and the Piazza Duomo beside it has frescoed Renaissance buildings and the Late Baroque Fountain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neptune">Neptune</a> of 1767 to 1768. The Tridentine Diocesan Museum, in the Palazzo Pretorio, shows the artistic treasures of the diocese and the influence of the council on the city. Since 2011 Trento has belonged to the Euroregion Tyrol-South Tyrol-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trento” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mercia, from the Old English Mierce, the 'border people', took shape among Anglian settlers in the upper <b>Trent</b> valley, around what later became Staffordshire, Derbyshire and Leicestershire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The annexation of <b>Trento</b> and Trieste in 1918 is often considered the culmination of the movement, and some historians see the Risorgimento as continuing to the Treaty of Rapallo in 1920.</blockquote><a href="/movement/unification-of-italy">Unification of Italy · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Catholic Church's council opened at <b>Trent</b> in northern Italy and met, with long breaks, until 1563.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-13">13 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts of Tyrol, named after Tyrol Castle near Merano, appear around 1140 as advocates of the bishoprics of Brixen and <b>Trent</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-tyrol">County of Tyrol</a></li><li><blockquote>Soon after the Lombard invasion of 568, a duchy was set up at <b>Tridentum</b> (Trento) to control the Adige valley, the main route from the Brenner Pass into the Po plain.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-trent">Duchy of Trent</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Trent</b> was a bishopric from the 4th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-trent">Prince-Bishopric of Trent</a></li></ul></section>
